  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&#x2019; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">On 16 June 2022, a new Decent Work Country Programme (DWCP), 2022-2026, was signed by the constituents including the Lao Federation of Trade Unions (LFTU). The new DWCP is guiding cooperation between the Lao People&#x2019;s Democratic Republic and the ILO. Before the launch, the LFTU conducted &#x2018;2022 Work Planning Workshop on Trade Unions&#x2019; role and Priorities in Implementing and Monitoring of DWCP&#x2019; on 7 June 2022, followed by &#x2018;Training on Foundation of Project Management&#x2019; on 8 and 9 June 2022. The LFTU understood the priorities of the new DWCP (2022-2026) and Trade Union&#x2019;s role, identified the priorities and drafted workplan of 2022 in accordance of identified priorities. Also, the LFTU representatives gained knowledge of project management, which was essential for achieving the LFTU objectives. The UN Resident Coordinator acknowledged their priorities on June 17, 2022. In 2022-2023, LFTU prioritized promoting collective bargaining through grassroots union capacity building and organizing unorganized workers.The LFTU conducted capacity building training on 27-28 April 2023 with attendance of 40 participants including provincial LFTU leaders of 6 provinces (Luang Prabang, Bokeo, Xayabouly, Borikhamxay and Savannakhet). The training adopted the detailed workplan to increase CBA. The LFTU formed a working group (WG) consisting of provincial LFTU leaders. The WG - supervised by the Central level LFTU - is regularlized to monitor the progress of initiative. WG members conducted a survey on CBAs. By the end of 2023, the number of CBA in the target provinces have increased. In addition to the above priority, the LFTU expressed strong need to extend the policy influence. LFTU entered into a framework agreement on bipartite social dialogue with the LNCCI in May 2022 and agreed in May 2023 to meet regularly to discuss the issues of labour legislation, skills development, and social protection. Both parties agreed to form a working committee, aiming to strengthen the mechanism. ILO provided the LFTU with the workers&apos; tools and materials for strengthening policy advocacy so that it can effectively channel the needs of workers to the employers and the government and participate in social dialogue.  It also provided technical support to increase capacity of leaders of the LFTU for better implementation of identified objectives. 
ILO conducted a series of consultations with the LFTU especially with the Labour Protection Department to draft priorities to increase the CBA and to organize more members in targeted provinces. 
ILO advised the LFTU on the significance and role of the LFTU to the DWCP as well as the UNSDCF to secure reflection of workers&#x2019; needs. 
For policy advocacy through social dialogue, ILO held bipartite social dialogue workshops in 2022 and 2023 together with LFTU to develop policy recommendations to the government.</narrative>
   </description>
